.TH FBSET 1 "July 25, 2026"
.UC 4
.SH NAME
fbset \- inspect or change a ReBSD DRM framebuffer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fbset
[
.I WIDTHxHEIGHTxBPP
]
.PP
.B fbset
[
.I width height bpp
]
.PP
.B fbset
.B \-l
.PP
.B fbset
.B fill
.I pixel-value
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fbset
queries or changes the active DRM framebuffer mode through
.IR /dev/fb0 .
It uses the same interface on every supported platform.
The utility also queries the Linux fbdev compatibility view and verifies that
its geometry, stride, physical address, and framebuffer length agree with the
native ReBSD DRM information.
.PP
With no arguments,
.B fbset
prints the current geometry, pixel format, framebuffer address, mode index,
pixel clock, and mapping information.
.PP
The
.B \-l
option lists every resolution/depth mode advertised by the kernel.  The active mode is marked
with an asterisk.
.PP
The
.B fill
form fills the current framebuffer with the supplied native pixel value
through the shared mapping returned by
.IR /dev/fb0 .
This is a direct mapping smoke test, not a write(2) transfer.
.PP
When the depth is omitted,
.B fbset
prefers the active depth and falls back to 16 bits.
The Ci20 driver obtains monitor modes through HDMI DDC/EDID and retains
640x480 XRGB8888 as a fallback.  The N64 driver exposes 320x240 RGBA5551 and
RGBA8888 modes, plus both depths at 640x480 interlaced with an Expansion Pak.
.SH EXAMPLES
Show the current framebuffer mode:
.PP
.RS
.nf
fbset
.fi
.RE
.PP
List the available modes:
.PP
.RS
.nf
fbset -l
.fi
.RE
.PP
Select an advertised 320x240 32-bit mode:
.PP
.RS
.nf
fbset 320x240x32
.fi
.RE
.PP
Fill an RGBA5551 framebuffer with blue, or an RGBA8888 framebuffer with red:
.PP
.RS
.nf
fbset fill 0x001f
fbset fill 0xff0000ff
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP 12
.I /dev/fb0
ReBSD DRM framebuffer device with Linux fbdev compatibility
